---
import ProseLayout from '../../layouts/ProseLayout.astro';
import ControllerIdentity from '../../components/privacy/ControllerIdentity.astro';
import ChildrensPrivacy from '../../components/privacy/ChildrensPrivacy.astro';
import PrivacyContact from '../../components/privacy/PrivacyContact.astro';
---

<ProseLayout
  title="GLANCElauncher Privacy Policy"
  description="How GLANCElauncher handles your data. It has no internet access: everything it reads stays on your phone, with no accounts, no analytics, and nothing sent to us."
  lastUpdated="November 6, 2026"
>
  <p class="lead">
    GLANCElauncher is a home screen app for Android. It does not collect, store
    or share your personal data. It has no internet permission, so it cannot
    send anything off your phone. This policy explains what GLANCElauncher reads
    on your phone, why, and what it keeps.
  </p>

  <ControllerIdentity>
    <p>
      This policy covers GLANCElauncher (Android package
      app.glance.launcher) specifically. For our publisher-level
      practices and the glance-apps.com website, see the{' '}
      <a href="/privacy">GLANCE Apps privacy policy</a>.
    </p>
  </ControllerIdentity>

  <h2>Data we do not collect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>We do not collect personal information.</li>
    <li>We do not use analytics, advertising or tracking.</li>
    <li>We do not have user accounts.</li>
    <li>
      GLANCElauncher has no internet permission. Neither the app nor anything
      built into it can send data off your phone, to us or to anyone else.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Data the app accesses</h2>
  <p>
    Everything below is used only on your phone, to show your home screen.
    Features marked optional are off until you turn them on, and the launcher
    works without them.
  </p>

  <h3>Installed apps</h3>
  <p>
    GLANCElauncher lists the apps installed on your phone so you can open,
    search, pin and uninstall them. Listing and opening apps is what a home
    screen does, which is why it can see every installed app.
  </p>

  <h3>App usage (optional)</h3>
  <p>
    If you grant usage access in Android's settings, the dock's recent apps can
    include apps you opened from elsewhere. GLANCElauncher reads which apps were
    used in the last two days, and nothing else.
  </p>

  <h3>Notification access (optional)</h3>
  <p>
    If you grant notification access, apps with unread notifications show a
    dot. GLANCElauncher checks which app posted a notification and whether it
    has any text. It does not keep or show what a notification says.
  </p>

  <h3>Contacts (optional)</h3>
  <p>
    With Contacts in search turned on, search can find your contacts.
    GLANCElauncher reads contact names, and a contact's phone number when you
    choose to call or text them from search. It asks for contacts permission
    only when you turn the setting on, and never changes your contacts.
  </p>

  <h3>Microphone (optional)</h3>
  <p>
    The microphone in the search bar turns what you say into a search.
    GLANCElauncher uses your phone's on-device speech recognition where the
    phone has it, and otherwise asks Android's speech recognizer to work
    offline. That recognizer is part of Android or another app on your phone,
    and its own privacy terms apply to it. GLANCElauncher receives only the
    text. It asks for microphone permission the first time you tap the
    microphone.
  </p>

  <h3>Accessibility service: double-tap to lock (optional)</h3>
  <p>
    With Double-tap to lock turned on, double-tapping an empty part of the home
    screen locks your phone. Android lets a home app lock the screen, with
    fingerprint and face unlock still working, only through an accessibility
    service, and GLANCElauncher's service does that one thing. It receives no
    accessibility events, cannot read what is on your screen or what you type,
    and collects nothing. GLANCElauncher explains this and asks before
    Android's Accessibility settings open, and you turn the service on there.
  </p>

  <h3>dayGLANCE and lastGLANCE</h3>
  <p>
    If you have dayGLANCE or lastGLANCE installed, GLANCElauncher shows your
    day, tasks and chores from them, and passes back what you do on the home
    screen, such as completing a task or adding one. This goes directly between
    the apps on your phone, through Android permissions that only the genuine
    GLANCE apps hold. What those apps do with it is covered by the{' '}
    <a href="/dayglance/privacy">dayGLANCE</a> and{' '}
    <a href="/lastglance/privacy">lastGLANCE</a> privacy policies.
  </p>

  <h3>Handing you to other apps</h3>
  <p>
    Some features open another app, which then works under its own privacy
    terms: web search opens your browser with your search, the clock opens your
    clock app, calling or texting a contact opens your dialer or messaging app,
    and a long press on the microphone opens dayGLANCE's voice capture.
  </p>

  <h2>Data stored on your device</h2>
  <p>
    GLANCElauncher keeps its settings (theme, layout, pinned apps and the like)
    and a short queue of home screen actions waiting for dayGLANCE or
    lastGLANCE to confirm them. Both stay in the app's private storage on your
    phone. Your platform's backup (Google Backup) may include them if you use
    it. Export settings saves your settings to a file in a place you choose;
    nothing is exported unless you do it. Uninstalling GLANCElauncher deletes
    everything it stored on your phone.
  </p>

  <h2>Third-party services</h2>
  <p>
    GLANCElauncher does not integrate with any third-party analytics,
    advertising or data brokers, and it connects to no service on the internet.
    The only other software involved is what is already on your phone: Android
    itself, its speech recognizer, and the apps you open from the launcher.
  </p>

  <ChildrensPrivacy />

  <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
  <p>
    If GLANCElauncher starts using data differently, we will update this policy
    and its "Last updated" date above first.
  </p>

  <PrivacyContact />
</ProseLayout>
